== To the Reader

CHRISTIAN READER:

#smallcaps[There] are two things that I have always looked on as difficult. The one is to make the #emph[wicked sad.] They do not lack cause for sadness: they are “taken captive” by Satan #footnote[#emph[2 Tim 2:25.]]. This is all that is gained by fighting on the devil’s side. He does not make his soldiers #emph[captains,] but #emph[captives.] He leads them as prisoners before him, and at last he will give them damnable pay. Yet sinners are so blinded by “the god of this age” #footnote[#emph[2 Cor 4:4.]] that they cannot see the chains they are held in, but kiss their fetters and go laughing to hell. The other puzzling difficulty is to make the #emph[godly joyful.] They have enough to rock their troubled hearts quiet, and may find strength “in the LORD their God”; yet like the peevish child they push away the breast and refuse to be comforted. #emph[Amnon,] though a king’s son, was lean #footnote[#emph[2 Sam 13:4.]]. This dejection in the godly arises from a double spring: either their inward comforts are darkened, or their outward comforts are disturbed. To cure both these troubles I have put out this piece that follows, hoping that by the blessing of God it will buoy up their despondent hearts and make them look with a more pleasant face. I would prescribe them to take a little of this #emph[cordial] now and then. When the prophet #emph[Elijah]’s spirits were ready to faint under the #emph[juniper tree,] the angel set before him a #emph[cake] and a #emph[jar of water,] and when he had eaten he was cheered, and went in the strength of that food until he came to #emph[Horeb, the mountain of God] #footnote[#emph[1 Kgs 19:8.]]. I think this text, like that sweet meal, may greatly strengthen and fortify the saints in their journey to heaven, and may be like a sacred feather to drop the golden oil of consolation into their hearts: “All things work together for good to those who love God.” To know that nothing will hurt the godly is a matter of comfort#footnote[#emph[Ps 91:10.]]\; but to be assured that all things that happen will cooperate for their good, that their crosses will be turned into blessings, that the bloody showers of affliction will water the withering root of their grace and make it flourish more: here is what may fill their hearts with joy until they run over. It will be no small revival to me if these labors of mine prove helpful to anyone. When St. #emph[Paul’s] prison would not allow him the liberty of a pulpit, he wrote some epistles, and the church of God has reaped their benefit in the ages since. Now that I am for the present taken off from my public work of preaching, I will be glad if I may be useful by writing, and while I myself am dead #emph[in a civil sense,] I may make others alive #emph[in a spiritual sense.] This is the prayer of him who is

#v(1em)

#align(right)[Your friend, in all true affection and devotion,]

#align(right)[THOMAS WATSON.]
